\begin_layout Title
Chapter 12
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*
Public Goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A 
\emph on
pure public good
\emph default
 is a good in which consuming one unit of the good by an individual in no
 way prevents others form consuming the same unit of of the good.
 Thus, the good is nonexcludable and non-rival.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

A good is 
\emph on
excludable
\emph default
 if people can be exlduded form consuming it.
 A good is 
\emph on
non-rival
\emph default
 if one person's consumption does not reduce the amount available to other
 consumers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\emph on
Examples of public goods
\emph default
: street lights, policemen, fire protection, highway system, national defense,
 public parks, etc.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset


\emph on
Local public goods
\emph default
: when there is a location restriction for the service of a public good.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Even if the competitive market is an efficient social institution for allocating
 private goods in an efficient manner, it turns out that a private market
 is not a very good mechanism for allocating public goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Notations and Basic Settings
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The general setting of a public goods economy includes consumers, producers,
 private goods, and public goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 : the number of consumers.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $L$
\end_inset

 : the number of private goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $K$
\end_inset

 : the number of public goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Z_{i}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{K}$
\end_inset

 : the consumption space of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Z\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{nL}\times\mathbb{R}_{+}^{K}$
\end_inset

 : consumption space.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 : a consumption of private goods by consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{K}$
\end_inset

 : a consumption/production of public goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $w_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 : the initial endowment of private goods for consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
 For simplicity, it is assumed that there is no public goods endowment,
 but they can be produced from private goods by a firm.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $v\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 : the private goods input.
 For simplicity, assume there is only one firm to produce the public goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}_{+}^{K}$
\end_inset

 : production funciton with 
\begin_inset Formula $y=f(v)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{i}$
\end_inset

 : the profit share of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from the production.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y)\in Z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{n},y)\in Z$
\end_inset

 : an allocation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\succsim_{i}$
\end_inset

 (or 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 if it exists) is a preference ordering.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}=(Z_{i},\succsim_{i},w_{i},\theta_{i})$
\end_inset

 : the characteristic of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $e=(e_{1},\ldots,e_{n},f)$
\end_inset

 : a public goods economy.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z\equiv(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is feasible if 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}+v\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
y=f(v)
\end{equation}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
Pareto efficient
\emph default
 for a public goods economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 if it is feasible and there is no other feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:(x_{i}^{\prime},y^{\prime})\succsim_{i}(x_{i},y)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k:(x_{k}^{\prime},y^{\prime})\succ_{k}(x_{k},y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is 
\emph on
weakly Pareto efficient
\emph default
 for the public goods economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 if it is feasible and there is no other feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:(x_{i}^{\prime},y)\succ_{i}(x_{i},y)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 12.2.1.

\series default
 Unlike private goods economies, even though under the assumptions of continuity
 and strict monotonicity, a weakly Pareto efficient allocation may not be
 Pareto efficient for the public goods economies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
12.2.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
For the public goods economies, a weakly Pareto efficient allocation may
 not be Pareto efficient even if preferences satisfy strict monotonicity
 and continuity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
The proof is by way of an example.
 Consider an economy with 
\begin_inset Formula $(n,L,K)=(3,1,1)$
\end_inset

, constant returns in producing 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 from 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 (the input-output coefficient normalized to one), and the following endowments
 and utility funcitons: 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{1}=w_{2}=w_{3}=1$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{1}(x_{1},y)=x_{1}+y$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i},y)=x_{i}+2y$
\end_inset

 for 
\begin_inset Formula $i=2,3$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $z=(x_{1},x_{2},x_{3},y)=(0.5,0,0,2.5)$
\end_inset

 is weakly Pareto efficient but not Pareto efficient because 
\begin_inset Formula $z^{\prime}=(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})=(0,0,0,3)$
\end_inset

 Pareto-dominates 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 by consumers 2 and 3.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

However, under an additional condition of strict convexity, they are equivalent
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
12.2.2.

\series default
 
\emph on
For the public goods economies, if preferences satisfy strict convexity,
 strict monotonicity, and continuity, and the production function is strictly
 increasing and concave, then weak Pareto efficiency implies Pareto efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Assume preferences satisfy strict monotonicity, strict convexity, and continuity.
 Also, assume the production function is strictly increasing and concave.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 be a weakly Pareto efficient allocation.
 Suppose for contradiction it is not Pareto efficient.
 Then, there exists a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{\prime},y^{\prime})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:(x_{i}^{\prime},y^{\prime})\succsim_{i}(x_{i},y)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k\in N:(x_{k}^{\prime},y^{\prime})\succ_{k}(x_{k},y)$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i\lambda}\equiv\lambda(x_{i}^{\prime},y^{\prime})+(1-\lambda)(x_{i},y),\ \lambda\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 By strict convexity of preferences, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ i\in N:z_{i\lambda}\succ_{i}(x_{i},y)
\]

\end_inset

Now, we will show that 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i\lambda}$
\end_inset

 is feasible.
 Since both allocations are feasible, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}+v & \leq & \sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}\\
\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}+v^{\prime} & \leq & \sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
f(v) & = & y\\
f(v^{\prime}) & = & y^{\prime}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Taking a linear combination of the two equations above for 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda\in(0,1)$
\end_inset

 we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\lambda\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}+\lambda v^{\prime}+(1-\lambda)\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}+(1-\lambda)v & = & \lambda\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}+(1-\lambda)\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}\\
\lambda\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{\prime}+\lambda v^{\prime}+(1-\lambda)\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}+(1-\lambda)v & = & w_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since the production function is concave, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
f(\lambda v^{\prime}+(1-\lambda)v) & \geq & \lambda f(v^{\prime})+(1-\lambda)f(v)\\
 & = & \lambda y^{\prime}+(1-\lambda)y
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i\lambda}$
\end_inset

 is a feasible allocation.
 Thus, we have a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i\lambda}$
\end_inset

 that is a strong Pareto improvement of 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

, i.e., all the consumers are made better off.
 However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is weakly Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Discrete Public Goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
12.3.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Efficient Provision of Public Goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For simplicity, consider a public good economy with 
\begin_inset Formula $n$
\end_inset

 consumers and two goods: one private good and one public good.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the contribution made by consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, so that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{i}+g_{i} & = & w_{i}\\
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}g_{i} & = & v
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}(x_{i},y)$
\end_inset

 is strictly monotonic increasing and continuous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 be the cost of producing the public project so that the production technology
 is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\begin{cases}
1 & \mbox{if}\ \sum_{i=1}^{n}g_{i}\geq c\\
0 & \mbox{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\]

\end_inset

We want to know under what conditions providing the public good will Pareto
 dominate not producing it, i.e., there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $(g_{1},\ldots,g_{n})$
\end_inset

 such that 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{n}g_{i}\geq c$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\forall\ i\in N:u_{i}(w_{i}-g_{i},1)>u_{i}(w_{i},0)
\]

\end_inset


\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the maximum willingness-to-pay (reservation price) of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 must satisfy
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{i}(w_{i}-r_{i},1)=u_{i}(w_{i},0)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

If producing the public project Pareto dominates not producing the public
 project, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\forall\ i\in N:u_{i}(w_{i}-g_{i},1)>u_{i}(w_{i},0)=u_{i}(w_{i}-r_{i},1)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

By monotonicity of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\forall\ i\in N:w_{i}-g_{i}>w_{i}-r_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Then, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
r_{i}>g_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}r_{i}>{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}g_{i}\geq c
\end{equation}

\end_inset

That is, the sum of the willingness-to-pay for the public good must exceed
 the cost of providing it.
 This condition is necessary.
 In fact, this condition is also sufficient.
 In summary, we have the following proposition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proposition*

\series bold
\shape up
12.3.1.

\series default
 
\emph on
Providing a public good Pareto dominates not producing the public good if
 and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{n}r_{i}>\sum_{i=1}^{n}g_{i}\geq c$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}$
\end_inset

 is defined to be the maximum willingness-to-pay (reservation price) of
 consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
12.3.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Free-Rider Problem
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

How effective is a private market at providing public goods? The answer
 as shown below is that we cannot expect that purely independent decision
 will necessarily result in an efficient amount of the public good being
 produce.
 To see this, suppose
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
r_{i} & = & 100,\ i=1,2\\
c & = & 150\ \mbox{(total cost)}\\
g_{i} & = & \begin{cases}
75 & \mbox{if both agents make contributions}\\
150 & \mbox{if only agent \ensuremath{i}\ makes a contribution}
\end{cases}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Each person decides independently whether or not to buy the public good.
 As a result, each one has an incentive to be a free-rider on the other.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Note that the net payoffs are defined by 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{i}-g_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, it is given by 
\begin_inset Formula $100-75=25$
\end_inset

 when both consumers are willing to produce the public project, and 
\begin_inset Formula $100-150=-50$
\end_inset

 when only one person wants to buy, but the other person does not.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

This is the prisoner's dilemma.
 The dominant strategy equilibrium in this game is (doesn't buy, doesn't
 buy).
 Thus, no body wants to share the cost of producing the public project,
 but wants to free-ride on the other consumer.
 As a result, the public good is not provided at all even though it would
 be efficient to do so.
 Therefore, voluntary contribution is general does not result in the efficient
 level of the public good.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
12.3.3
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Voting for a Discrete Public Good
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The amount of a public good is often determined by voting.
 Will this generally result in an efficient provision? The answer is no.
 Voting does not result in efficient provision.
 Consider the following example.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
12.3.1.

\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
c=99\\
r_{1}=90,\ r_{2}=30,\ r_{3}=30
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

Clearly, 
\begin_inset Formula $r_{1}+r_{2}+r_{3}>c$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g_{i}=\nicefrac{99}{3}=33$
\end_inset

.
 So, the efficient provision of the public good should be yes.
 However, under the majority rule, only consumer 1 votes 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

yes
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 since she receives a positive net benefit if the good is provided.
 The 2nd and 3rd persons vote 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

no
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 to produce the public good, and therefore, the public good will not be
 provided so that we have inefficient provision of the public good.
 The problem with the majority rule is that it only measures the net benefit
 for the public good, whereas the efficient condition requires a comparison
 of willingness-to-pay.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
12.4
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Continuous Public Goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
12.4.1
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Efficient Provision of Public Goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Again, for simplicity, we assume there is only one public good and one private
 good that may be regarded as money, and 
\begin_inset Formula $y=f(v)$
\end_inset

.
 The welfare maximization approach shows that Pareto efficient allocations
 can be characterized by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\max_{(x,y)} & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\alpha_{i}u_{i}(x_{i},y)\\
\mbox{s.t.} & {\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}+v\leq{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}w_{i}\\
 & y=f(v)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Define the Lagrangian function
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
L=\sum_{i=1}^{n}\alpha_{i}u_{i}(x_{i},y)+\lambda\left({\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}w_{i}-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}-v\right)+\mu\left(f(v)-y\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

When 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 is strictly quasi-concave and differentiable and 
\begin_inset Formula $f(v)$
\end_inset

 is concave and differentiable, the set of interior Pareto optimal allocations
 are characterized by the first order conditions:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{i}}=0:\alpha_{i}\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}=\lambda\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial v}=0:\mu f^{\prime}(v)=\lambda\\
\frac{\partial L}{\partial y}=0:{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\alpha_{i}\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y}=\mu
\end{gather}

\end_inset

By equations (9) and (10)
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
\frac{\alpha_{i}}{\mu}=\frac{f^{\prime}(v)}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Substituting (12) into (11),
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}\frac{f^{\prime}(v)}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}}\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y}=1}\nonumber \\
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}}=\frac{1}{f^{\prime}(v)}
\end{gather}

\end_inset

Thus, we obtain the well known Lindahl-Samuelson condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In conclusion, the conditions for Pareto efficiency are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather}
\left\{ \begin{array}{c}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}MRS_{yx_{i}}=\frac{1}{f^{\prime}(v)}\\
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}+v\leq{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}w_{i}\\
y=f(v)
\end{array}\right.
\end{gather}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
12.4.1.
\series default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{i} & = & \alpha_{i}\ln y+\ln x_{i}\\
y & = & v
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

the Lindahl-Samuelson condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}}=1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and so,
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\frac{\frac{\alpha_{i}}{y}}{\frac{1}{x_{i}}}={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\frac{\alpha_{i}x_{i}}{y}=1\implies{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\alpha_{i}x_{i}=y
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which implies the level of the public good is not uniquely determined.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

In general, the marginal willingness-to-pay for a public good depends on
 the amount of private goods consumption.
 Therefore, the efficient level of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 depends on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 However, in the case of quasi-linear utility functions
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
u_{i}(x_{i},y)=x_{i}+u_{i}(y)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

the Lindahl-Samuelson condition becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}u_{i}^{\prime}(y)=\frac{1}{f^{\prime}(v)}\equiv c^{\prime}(y)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

so 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 is uniquely determined.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
12.4.2.
\series default

\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{i} & = & \alpha_{i}\ln y+x_{i}\\
y & = & v
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

the Lindahl-Samuelson condition is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}}=1
\end{equation}

\end_inset

and so
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\frac{\alpha_{i}}{y}=1\implies{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}\alpha_{i}=y
\end{equation}

\end_inset

which implies the level of the public good is uniquely determined.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
12.4.2
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Lindahl Equilibrium
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We have given the conditions for Pareto efficiency in the presence of public
 goods.
 The next problem is how to achieve a Pareto efficient allocation in a decentral
ized way.
 In private-goods-only economies, any competitive equilibrium is Pareto
 optimal.
 However, with public goods, a competitive mechansim does not help.
 For instance, if we tried the competitive solution with a public good and
 two consumers, the utility maximization would equalize the MRS and the
 relative price, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
MRS_{yx}^{A}=MRS_{yx}^{B}=\frac{p_{y}}{p_{x}}
\]

\end_inset

This is an immediate violation to the Samuelson-Lindahl optimal condition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Lindahl suggested to use a tax method to provide a public good.
 Each person is assigned a specific 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

personalized price
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 for the public good.
 The Lindahl solution is a way to mimic the competitive solution in the
 presence of public goods.
 Suppose we devise a mechanism to allocate the production cost of a public
 good between consumers, then we can achieve the Samuelson-Lindahl condition.
 To this end, we apply different prices of a public good to differenct consumers.
 The idea of the Lindahl solution is that the consumption level of a public
 good is the same to all consumers, but the price of the public good is
 personalized among consumers in the way that the price ratio of two goods
 for each person equal the marginal rates of substitutions of two goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider a public goods economy 
\begin_inset Formula $e$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 (private goods) and 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{K}$
\end_inset

 (public goods).
 For simplicity, we assume CRS for 
\begin_inset Formula $y=f(v)$
\end_inset

.
 A feasible allocation is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}x_{i}+v\leq{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}w_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{K}$
\end_inset

 be the personalized price vector of consumer 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 for consuming the public goods.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $q=\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the market price vector of 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 be the price vector of private goods
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The profit is define as 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi=qy-pv$
\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $y=f(v)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Definition*

\series bold
12.4.1 (Lindahl Equilibrium Allocation).

\series default
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{nL+K}$
\end_inset

 is a Lindahl equilibrium allocation if it is feasible and there exists
 a price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

 and personalized price vectors 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}^{*}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{K}$
\end_inset

, one for each individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, such that
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $p^{*}x_{i}^{*}+q_{i}^{*}y^{*}\leq p^{*}w_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y)\succ_{i}(x_{i}^{*},y^{*})\implies p^{*}x_{i}+q_{i}^{*}y>p^{*}w_{i}$
\end_inset

 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $q^{*}y^{*}-p^{*}v^{*}=0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Definition*
where 
\begin_inset Formula $v^{*}=\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}-\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q=\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Remark 12.4.1.

\series default
 We call 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*},p^{*},q_{1}^{*},\ldots,q_{n}^{*})$
\end_inset

 a Lindahl equilibrium.
 Because of CRS, the maximum profit is zero at the Lindahl equilibrium.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Similarly, we have the following First Fundamental Theorem of Welfare Economics
 for public goods economies.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Theorem*

\series bold
\shape up
12.4.1.
 
\series default
\emph on
Every Lindahl equilibrium allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 with price system 
\begin_inset Formula $(p^{*},q_{1}^{*},\ldots,q_{n}^{*})$
\end_inset

 is weakly Pareto efficient, and further, under local non-satiation, it
 is Pareto efficient.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Proof
Because the first part is simple, we only prove the second part.
 Suppose for contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 is not Pareto effiicient.
 Then, there exists a feasible allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 such that
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
\forall\ i\in N:(x_{i},y)\succsim_{i}(x_{i}^{*},y^{*})\\
\exists\ k\in N:(x_{k},y)\succ_{k}(x_{k}^{*},y)
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

By local non-satiation, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ i\in N:p^{*}x_{i}+q_{i}^{*}y\geq p^{*}w_{i}$
\end_inset

, and by the definition of a Lindahl Equilibrium, 
\begin_inset Formula $\exists\ k\in N:p^{*}x_{k}+q_{k}^{*}y>p^{*}w_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Summing over all the agents we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}p^{*}x_{i}+{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}q_{i}^{*}y>{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p^{*}w_{i}}\\
\sum_{i=1}^{n}p^{*}x_{i}+q^{*}y>{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}p^{*}w_{i}
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

By profit maximization, 
\begin_inset Formula $q^{*}y-p^{*}v\leq q^{*}y^{*}-p^{*}v^{*}=0\implies q^{*}y\leq p^{*}v$
\end_inset

.
 Thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle p^{*}\left[\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}+v-{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}w_{i}\right]>0}
\]

\end_inset

However, this contradicts the fact that 
\begin_inset Formula $(x,y)$
\end_inset

 is feasible.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

For a public economy with one private good and one public good 
\begin_inset Formula $y=\frac{1}{q}v$
\end_inset

, the definition of a Lindahl equilibrium becomes much simpler.
 An allocation 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 is a Lindahl equilibrium allocation if 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 is feasible, i.e., 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{n}x_{i}^{*}+q^{*}y^{*}\leq\sum_{i=1}^{n}w_{i}$
\end_inset

, and there exists 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $i=1,\ldots,n$
\end_inset

, such that
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}^{*}+q_{i}^{*}y\leq w_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},y)\succ_{i}(x_{i}^{*},y^{*})\implies x_{i}+q_{i}^{*}y>w_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{i=1}^{n}q_{i}^{*}=q^{*}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In fact, the feasibility condition is automatically satisfied when the budget
 constraints given by (i) are satisfied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

If 
\begin_inset Formula $(x^{*},y^{*})$
\end_inset

 is an interior Lindahl equilibrium allocation, then from utility maximization,
 we can have the first-order condition:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial y}}{\frac{\partial u_{i}}{\partial x_{i}}}=q_{i}
\]

\end_inset

which means the Lindahl-Samuelson condition holds
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
{\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}MRS_{yx_{i}}=q
\]

\end_inset

which is the necessary condition for Pareto efficiency.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*

\series bold
12.4.3.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
u_{i}(x_{i},y) & = & x_{i}^{\alpha_{i}}y^{(1-\alpha_{i})};\ 0<\alpha_{i}<1\\
y & = & \frac{1}{q}v
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\series default
The budget constraint is 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
x_{i}+q_{i}y=w_{i}
\]

\end_inset

For each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, the demand functions for 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 are given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{i} & = & \alpha_{i}w_{i}\\
y_{i} & = & \frac{(1-\alpha_{i})w_{i}}{q_{i}}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}=\cdots=y_{n}=y^{*}$
\end_inset

 at equilibrium, then we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{i}y^{*}=(1-\alpha_{i})w_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Summing over 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula 
\[
qy^{*}={\displaystyle \sum_{i=1}^{n}}(1-\alpha_{i})w_{i}
\]

\end_inset

So, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y^{*}=\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{n}(1-\alpha_{i})w_{i}}{q}
\]

\end_inset

and thus,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{i}=\frac{(1-\alpha_{i})w_{i}}{y^{*}}=\frac{q(1-\alpha_{i})w_{i}}{\sum_{i=1}^{n}(1-\alpha_{i})w_{i}}
\]

\end_inset

If we want to find a Lindahl equilibrium, we must know the preferences or
 MRS of each consumer.
\end_layout

